d  on building up state affiliates in all  50 states in the District of Columbia  and frankly we're just better at this  ballot access thing than all the other  parties talk about what it is required  to get to make that happen how much of  your energy is spent just making sure  that you develop this 50 state prize  over the last 45 years we've put  millions of dollars into breaking down  ballot access barriers into complying  with the petition requirements that the  two old parties put in place to try and  suppress the libertarian vote  this year and we're in a great ballot  access spot we're likely to spend about  three hundred and fifty thousand dollars  just to give Americans a choice now what  have you seen in terms of numbers of  people visiting or memberships or other  things that would anecdotally or you  know put numbers on the support I've  seen recently memberships and revenue  are both skyrocketing we have been  inundated with people joining the party  memberships up about 12% just since the  cruise drop out daily memberships have  doubled or tripled revenue we've  quadrupled what we normally bring it in  a month and we're on track to continue  doing that as we go into the election  and it'll just get better once Sanders  gets boxed out by that corporate shell  so one of the bigger complaints that you  hear from people about the Libertarian  Party is that how come there isn't a  libertarian senator or how come you  don't have people in local office what's  your response to the response is this is  always an argument in the party do we do  we run top of the ticket to Iran local  races the answer is we can walk and chew  gum at the same time the National  Committee has a goal of having over a  thousand candidates up and down the  ballot nationwide in 2016 and there's a  coattail effect both directions  presidential candidates do better when  there are local candidates running and  vice versa so our strategy is to do both  what is the goal there's a lot of 